1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:04,489
Open the intended record and verify its
identity before making a correction.

2
00:00:04,929 --> 00:00:09,630
This Training example is Finalized, with
record number T R N, twenty

3
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:15,829
twenty-six, zero zero zero two, and draft
reference D, Y X S nine three.

4
00:00:16,229 --> 00:00:20,520
Its Course entry ends with the code
Academy, R E C, twenty-nine.

5
00:00:21,139 --> 00:00:24,290
Read the recorded author, group and
narrative as well.

6
00:00:24,610 --> 00:00:27,780
This is explicitly a fictional software
exercise.

7
00:00:28,299 --> 00:00:31,110
Once you have confirmed the intended
record and your authority

8
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:33,270
to correct it, choose Amend.

9
00:00:33,519 --> 00:00:38,030
The editor identifies the same Training
record and displays an important notice:

10
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:43,059
an amendment is in progress, and the last
finalized revision remains authoritative

11
00:00:43,099 --> 00:00:44,969
until the amendment is finalized.

12
00:00:45,379 --> 00:00:47,370
Read that distinction before editing.

13
00:00:47,870 --> 00:00:53,940
Station slash group, External ID, dates,
Course, Course code, Location

14
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:55,519
and Narrative are shown here.

15
00:00:56,009 --> 00:01:00,419
Opening this editor has not, by itself,
replaced the finalized version

16
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:02,069
with a newly signed correction.

17
00:01:02,319 --> 00:01:04,640
Edit only the field that needs correction.

18
00:01:05,069 --> 00:01:10,169
Here, Course code changes from Academy, R
E C, twenty-nine, to

19
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,920
Academy, R E C, twenty-nine A.

20
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:18,149
The Course title, fictional workspace and
software-only narrative remain unchanged.

21
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:22,209
A focused correction is easier to review
later than an unexplained

22
00:01:22,209 --> 00:01:24,219
rewrite of unrelated details.

23
00:01:24,639 --> 00:01:27,670
Check the new field value and the
amendment notice before

24
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:29,199
moving to the save controls.

25
00:01:29,399 --> 00:01:33,350
At the bottom of the editor, review Reason
code, Reason and

26
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:34,939
the available save actions.

27
00:01:35,289 --> 00:01:38,439
This example uses Training Correction as
its reason code.

28
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:40,899
The attestation is still unchecked.

29
00:01:41,269 --> 00:01:45,419
Choose Save draft to retain the working
correction without finalizing it.

30
00:01:45,849 --> 00:01:48,140
Save and finalize is a separate action.

31
00:01:48,489 --> 00:01:52,689
The complete reason and attestation will
be reviewed again in the finalization

32
00:01:52,689 --> 00:01:54,730
panel before the amendment is signed.

33
00:01:55,019 --> 00:01:58,269
The reopened record shows the corrected
Course entry ending

34
00:01:58,269 --> 00:02:02,289
in twenty-nine A, while the
amendment-in-progress notice remains.

35
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:06,020
Its existing Finalized badge does not
remove that warning.

36
00:02:06,579 --> 00:02:10,569
Read the two together: a saved working
correction and the authoritative

37
00:02:10,569 --> 00:02:12,599
finalized revision are distinct.

38
00:02:13,029 --> 00:02:17,619
Edit and Abandon amendment are available
above, and the separate Finalize panel

39
00:02:17,669 --> 00:02:19,750
asks for a reason and attestation.

40
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:24,070
This example continues with the intended
correction rather than abandoning it.

41
00:02:24,349 --> 00:02:29,120
In Finalize, the reason code is Training
Correction and the full Reason says,

42
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,430
Correct fictional course code only.

43
00:02:31,899 --> 00:02:36,080
Read the attestation before selecting it:
the record must be accurate and

44
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,920
complete to the best of your knowledge.

45
00:02:38,210 --> 00:02:42,930
This recorded attestation concerns the
explicitly fictional software record, with

46
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:45,299
no physical training or response claimed.

47
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:49,729
Once the correction, reason and
attestation are appropriate, choose

48
00:02:49,729 --> 00:02:51,740
Finalize to complete this amendment.

49
00:02:51,910 --> 00:02:55,629
After finalization, the reopened record is
marked Amended.

50
00:02:56,090 --> 00:02:59,350
The record number and draft reference are
retained, and the Course

51
00:02:59,379 --> 00:03:01,650
entry still ends in twenty-nine A.

52
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:05,930
Verify this actual returned state rather
than assuming that an earlier

53
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:07,779
draft save completed the amendment.

54
00:03:08,159 --> 00:03:11,710
Keeping the record's identity while
retaining its revision history makes

55
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:14,950
a correction easier for another authorized
reviewer to follow.

56
00:03:15,149 --> 00:03:17,860
In History, read the numbered transitions.

57
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:22,639
Revision two is Amended with the Training
Correction reason code; revision

58
00:03:22,639 --> 00:03:24,649
one remains Finalized beneath it.

59
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:27,460
The rows identify the actor and date.

60
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:31,790
Use Compare with previous on the amended
row to review what changed

61
00:03:31,790 --> 00:03:33,730
between those two saved versions.

62
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:37,669
Verify the intended row before choosing
the comparison action.

63
00:03:37,899 --> 00:03:43,129
Revision comparison explicitly says
Revision one compared with revision two.

64
00:03:43,689 --> 00:03:47,140
Read Field, Before and After across the
same row.

65
00:03:47,569 --> 00:03:52,260
Details dot Course Code changes from
Academy, R E C, twenty-nine,

66
00:03:52,489 --> 00:03:54,680
to the version ending twenty-nine A.

67
00:03:55,069 --> 00:03:58,350
This comparison shows one changed field
for this correction.

68
00:03:58,729 --> 00:04:02,190
Compare the actual values with the stated
reason before drawing

69
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:03,889
a conclusion about the amendment.

70
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:09,350
This walkthrough is reading the comparison
page; it does not perform a PDF export.

71
00:04:09,570 --> 00:04:13,590
Return to the record and find revision
two's Amended row in History.

72
00:04:14,039 --> 00:04:17,300
Choose View on that row to inspect its
saved content.

73
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:21,709
This is a different question from the
comparison: the comparison identifies

74
00:04:21,709 --> 00:04:26,499
differences, while View opens the selected
revision's complete rendered record.

75
00:04:26,849 --> 00:04:30,229
Keep the row number and transition
together so you open the version

76
00:04:30,229 --> 00:04:32,140
you actually intend to inspect.

77
00:04:32,310 --> 00:04:36,919
The opened page identifies the department,
record number and revision two.

78
00:04:37,419 --> 00:04:42,189
Under Details, Course Code contains the
corrected value ending twenty-nine A.

79
00:04:42,559 --> 00:04:46,510
The fictional location and truthful
software-only narrative are retained.

80
00:04:46,969 --> 00:04:51,289
Attachments lists the recorded file
metadata, including the exercise handout.

81
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:55,649
Read the revision heading before treating
a saved page as the current version

82
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:57,680
or comparing it with another copy.

83
00:04:57,890 --> 00:05:01,629
Return to History again and find revision
one's Finalized row.

84
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,180
Choose View on that original row.

85
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:08,560
The presence of an amended revision above
it has not removed this earlier

86
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:10,190
saved version from the history.

87
00:05:10,579 --> 00:05:14,590
Selecting the correct row lets an
authorized reviewer inspect the original

88
00:05:14,590 --> 00:05:16,989
content alongside the later correction.

89
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:21,650
The original page identifies revision one
and retains the earlier Course Code

90
00:05:21,679 --> 00:05:24,159
ending twenty-nine, without the added A.

91
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:28,809
Its software-exercise narrative and
attachment metadata are still present.

92
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:32,840
Reading both saved versions and their
comparison makes the correction

93
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:37,429
traceable: you can identify the original
value, the amended value and

94
00:05:37,429 --> 00:05:41,400
the stated correction reason without
rewriting the earlier saved snapshot.

95
00:05:41,650 --> 00:05:45,399
The same careful correction and revision
checks can support authorized

96
00:05:45,399 --> 00:05:49,569
documentation in community paramedicine or
mental health response teams,

97
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:54,429
search-and-rescue organizations, security
companies, wildland contractors

98
00:05:54,429 --> 00:05:58,669
and industrial emergency teams, as well as
Fire and EMS departments.

99
00:05:59,249 --> 00:06:02,909
Use a specific reason, keep the
attestation truthful and

100
00:06:02,909 --> 00:06:04,440
inspect the saved result.

101
00:06:04,960 --> 00:06:10,309
A working draft, a finalized amendment, a
comparison and an earlier revision each

102
00:06:10,309 --> 00:06:14,629
serve a different purpose when explaining
what a record said and how it changed.

